1. **A lithography method comprising:**

   obtaining topography data representative of a height variation across a surface of a substrate;

   determining, based on the topography data, a predicted overlay error resulting from interaction between the height variation and a telecentricity characteristic of a radiation beam projected onto the substrate;

   determining a telecentricity adjustment configured to at least partially compensate for the predicted overlay error;

   adjusting a projection system in accordance with the telecentricity adjustment to change an angle of incidence of a chief ray of the projected radiation beam relative to the surface of the substrate; and

   projecting, using the adjusted projection system, a patterned radiation beam onto a radiation-sensitive layer of the substrate such that a lateral displacement caused by the changed angle of incidence at least partially offsets the predicted overlay error.

Below is illustrative claim language for attorney review and adaptation to the supporting specification.

### Claims

1. **A method of patterning a wafer by optical lithography, the method comprising:**

   obtaining topographic data representing a curvature of a surface of the wafer across a plurality of spatial locations;

   determining, from the topographic data, a local surface orientation for each of the plurality of spatial locations;

   calculating a spatial telecentricity-correction map based at least in part on the local surface orientations, the spatial telecentricity-correction map specifying, as a function of position on the wafer, a correction to a propagation direction of patterning radiation;

   controlling an optical projection system in accordance with the spatial telecentricity-correction map such that the patterning radiation is incident at respective, locally selected net propagation directions at different ones of the plurality of spatial locations; and

   exposing a radiation-sensitive layer on the wafer using the patterning radiation, thereby reducing an overlay error attributable to the curvature of the wafer.

2. The method of claim 1, wherein obtaining the topographic data comprises measuring a height of the wafer surface at each of the plurality of spatial locations using an optical, capacitive, interferometric, or acoustic sensor.

3. The method of claim 1, wherein determining the local surface orientation comprises calculating a surface-normal vector from a spatial gradient of the topographic data.

4. The method of claim 1, wherein calculating the spatial telecentricity-correction map comprises determining, for each spatial location, an angular correction that compensates for a difference between a local surface-normal vector and a nominal surface-normal vector.

5. The method of claim 1, wherein the spatial telecentricity-correction map further accounts for a non-telecentricity characteristic of a projection lens.

6. The method of claim 1, wherein the locally selected net propagation direction at a given spatial location is selected to reduce a lateral displacement between:

   a projected feature at a first elevation within the radiation-sensitive layer; and

   a corresponding feature at a second elevation associated with a previously formed layer of the wafer.

7. The method of claim 1, wherein controlling the optical projection system comprises adjusting at least one of:

   an illumination pupil distribution;

   a position of an illumination source;

   an angle of an illumination beam;

   an optical element of a projection lens;

   a spatial light modulator;

   a deformable mirror;

   a tiltable optical element; or

   a pose of a wafer stage.

8. The method of claim 1, wherein exposing the radiation-sensitive layer comprises scanning the wafer relative to a patterned radiation field, and wherein the locally selected net propagation directions are varied during the scanning based on a current exposure position.

9. The method of claim 1, further comprising:

   obtaining overlay metrology data from at least one previously patterned wafer;

   updating the spatial telecentricity-correction map based on the overlay metrology data; and

   exposing the wafer using the updated spatial telecentricity-correction map.

10. The method of claim 1, wherein calculating the spatial telecentricity-correction map comprises constraining the locally selected net propagation directions according to at least one of a numerical aperture, a depth of focus, a critical-dimension tolerance, an imaging-contrast threshold, or an actuation range of the optical projection system.

11. The method of claim 1, wherein the spatial telecentricity-correction map specifies respective two-dimensional angular vectors for different exposure-field locations.

12. The method of claim 1, wherein the topographic data are obtained for individual exposure fields, and wherein a respective telecentricity correction is calculated for each individual exposure field.

13. The method of claim 1, wherein the topographic data include wafer bow, wafer warp, or a local height variation caused by an underlying device structure.

14. The method of claim 1, wherein controlling the optical projection system causes a chief ray of the patterning radiation to have the locally selected net propagation direction.

15. The method of claim 1, wherein the locally selected net propagation direction is determined from a combination of an illumination direction, a projection-system telecentricity error, and a local orientation of the wafer surface.

16. **An optical lithography system comprising:**

   a wafer stage configured to support a wafer;

   a metrology subsystem configured to obtain topographic measurements representing curvature of a surface of the wafer;

   an illumination subsystem configured to provide patterning radiation;

   a projection subsystem configured to project the patterning radiation onto a radiation-sensitive layer of the wafer;

   at least one angular-control element configured to vary a net propagation direction of the patterning radiation as a function of position on the wafer; and

   a controller configured to:

   determine local surface orientations from the topographic measurements;

   generate a spatial telecentricity-correction map based at least in part on the local surface orientations; and

   operate the angular-control element in accordance with the spatial telecentricity-correction map so that different wafer locations are exposed using respective, locally selected net propagation directions that reduce curvature-induced overlay error.

17. The optical lithography system of claim 16, wherein the angular-control element comprises at least one of a pupil-plane modulator, a beam-steering mirror, a deformable mirror, an adjustable lens element, a source-position actuator, or a stage-tilt actuator.

18. The optical lithography system of claim 16, wherein the controller is configured to synchronize variation of the net propagation direction with scanning motion of the wafer stage.

19. The optical lithography system of claim 16, wherein the controller is further configured to predict a lateral pattern-placement error using the local surface orientations and a vertical separation between an alignment-reference plane and an exposure plane.

20. The optical lithography system of claim 16, wherein the controller is configured to select the respective net propagation directions by minimizing an objective function that includes predicted overlay error and at least one imaging-performance penalty.

21. **A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors of an optical lithography system, cause the optical lithography system to:**

   receive a wafer-surface map representing wafer curvature;

   derive a spatial distribution of local wafer-surface orientations from the wafer-surface map;

   calculate a spatial telecentricity-correction field that associates wafer positions with respective target propagation-direction vectors;

   command one or more components of the optical lithography system to establish the target propagation-direction vectors at corresponding wafer positions; and

   expose a pattern onto a radiation-sensitive layer while spatially varying a net direction of patterning radiation in accordance with the spatial telecentricity-correction field.

22. The non-transitory computer-readable medium of claim 21, wherein the instructions further cause the optical lithography system to revise the spatial telecentricity-correction field in response to measured overlay residuals.

23. The non-transitory computer-readable medium of claim 21, wherein calculating the spatial telecentricity-correction field comprises applying a regularization constraint that limits a spatial rate of change of the target propagation-direction vectors.

1. **A method of compensating for overlay error in a lithographic apparatus, the method comprising:**

   determining a substrate topology representing a surface-height variation of a curved substrate over an exposure region;

   obtaining a telecentricity characteristic of a projection system of the lithographic apparatus;

   calculating, from the substrate topology and the telecentricity characteristic, a spatially varying telecentricity profile indicative of lateral image displacement caused by projection of radiation onto respective surface heights of the curved substrate;

   deriving an overlay-correction profile based on the spatially varying telecentricity profile;

   controlling at least one of a substrate stage, a patterning-device stage, the projection system, or a pattern applied to a radiation beam in accordance with the overlay-correction profile; and

   projecting patterned radiation through the projection system onto the curved substrate while applying said control, thereby compensating for overlay error attributable to the substrate surface-height variation.
